WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.299 --> 00:00:04.620
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:09.380
Now that facial recognition is becoming more
and more of a hot topic, let's talk a bit

00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:10.880
about 3D face reconstruction!

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:16.330
This is a problem where we have a 2D input
photograph, or a video of a person, and the

00:00:16.330 --> 00:00:20.070
goal is to create a piece of 3D geometry from
it.

00:00:20.070 --> 00:00:26.069
To accomplish this, previous works often required
a combination of proper alignment of the face,

00:00:26.069 --> 00:00:31.509
multiple photographs and dense correspondences,
which is a fancy name for additional data

00:00:31.509 --> 00:00:34.890
that identifies the same regions across these
photographs.

00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:39.740
But this new formulation is the holy grail
of all possible versions of this problem,

00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:44.000
because it requires nothing else but one 2D
photograph.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:48.980
The weapon of choice for this work was a Convolutional
Neural Network, and the dataset the algorithm

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:54.679
was trained on couldn't be simpler: it was
given a large database of 2D input image and

00:00:54.679 --> 00:00:57.179
3D output geometry pairs.

00:00:57.179 --> 00:01:01.789
This means that the neural network can look
at a lot of these pairs and learn how these

00:01:01.789 --> 00:01:05.170
input photographs are mapped to 3D geometry.

00:01:05.170 --> 00:01:10.290
And as you can see, the results are absolutely
insane, especially given the fact that it

00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:16.410
works for arbitrary face positions and many
different expressions, and even with occlusions.

00:01:16.410 --> 00:01:21.340
However, this is not your classical Convolutional
Neural Network, because as we mentioned, the

00:01:21.340 --> 00:01:24.860
input is 2D and the output is 3D.

00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:30.240
So the question immediately arises: what kind
of data structure should be used for the output?

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:35.780
The authors went for a 3D voxel array, which
is essentially a cube in which we build up

00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:39.200
the face from small, identical Lego pieces.

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:44.070
This representation is similar to the terrain
in the game Minecraft, only the resolution

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:46.030
of these blocks is finer.

00:01:46.030 --> 00:01:50.890
The process of guessing how these voxel arrays
should look based the input photograph is

00:01:50.890 --> 00:01:54.940
referred to in the research community as volumetric
regression.

00:01:54.940 --> 00:01:56.750
This is what this work is about.

00:01:56.750 --> 00:01:58.800
And now comes the best part!

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:04.450
An online demo is also available where we
can either try some prepared images, or, we

00:02:04.450 --> 00:02:06.040
can also upload our own.

00:02:06.040 --> 00:02:10.470
So while I run my own experiments, don't leave
me out of the good stuff and make sure you

00:02:10.470 --> 00:02:12.860
post your results in the comments section!

00:02:12.860 --> 00:02:16.710
The source code is also available for you
fellow tinkerers out there.

00:02:16.710 --> 00:02:21.390
The limitations of this technique includes
the inability of detecting expressions that

00:02:21.390 --> 00:02:26.410
are very far away from the ones seen in the
training set, and as you can see in the videos,

00:02:26.410 --> 00:02:28.830
temporal coherence could also use some help.

00:02:28.830 --> 00:02:33.450
This means that if we have video input, the
reconstruction has some tiny differences in

00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:34.450
each frame.

00:02:34.450 --> 00:02:39.170
Maybe a Recurrent Neural Network, like some
variant of Long Short Term Memory could address

00:02:39.170 --> 00:02:40.710
this in the near future.

00:02:40.710 --> 00:02:44.920
However, those are trickier and more resource-intensive
to train properly.

00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:49.460
Very excited to see how these solutions evolve,
and of course, Two Minute Papers is going

00:02:49.460 --> 00:02:53.100
to be here for you to talk about some amazing
upcoming works.

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:57.680
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

